<h2>A Childhood Spent Where the Regiment Was</h2>

<p>He was born on 5 July 1803 at East Dereham in Norfolk. His father, Thomas Borrow, was a lieutenant in the West Norfolk Militia and a recruiting officer, which in practice meant the family moved wherever the regiment was sent. George spent his ninth and tenth years in the barracks at Norman Cross, a prisoner-of-war camp holding French soldiers, and the sounds of that place - French, and the accents of half of Europe - were part of his childhood before he could read any of it.</p>

<p>Then, in the autumn of 1815, the regiment went to Clonmel in Ireland, and the boy was enrolled at Clonmel Endowed School. It had one master, a Protestant clergyman called Richard Carey, whom Borrow later described as "a nice old clergyman", and Carey taught him to read Latin and Greek. Note the shape of that education: two classical languages, one teacher, a great deal of parsing, and no expectation that anybody would ever speak them.</p>

<h2>Irish for a Pack of Cards</h2>

<p>The Irish came from somewhere else entirely, and it is the most charming transaction in the history of language learning.</p>

<p>A fellow pupil named Murtagh spoke Irish, and Murtagh was miserable. He could not manage Greek, which meant he could not follow the class, which meant his days were empty and his holidays worse. Borrow had a pack of cards, new, never used. Murtagh had no money and plenty of time. The deal they struck was that Murtagh would teach Borrow Irish in exchange for the cards, and Borrow recorded the negotiation in <em>Lavengro</em>:</p>

<blockquote>"Yes, you speak Irish; I heard you talking it the other day to the cripple. You shall teach me Irish."<br /><br />"And is it a language-master you'd be making of me?"<br /><br />"To be sure! - what better can you do? - it would help you to pass your time at school. You can't learn Greek, so you must teach Irish!"</blockquote>

<p>And then the sentence that ought to be printed above every language school's door: <em>"Before Christmas, Murtagh was playing at cards with his brother Denis, and I could speak a considerable quantity of broken Irish."</em></p>

<p>Consider what that arrangement actually required. No textbook. No grammar table. No curriculum, no homework, no examination. One fluent speaker, one motivated learner, a shared activity, and a period of weeks. Murtagh's motive was cards; Borrow's was curiosity and a school where he was bored. It worked because the learner had daily access to a speaker who wanted something from him, which is a better arrangement than most classrooms manage with far more resources.</p>

<h2>Welsh Instead of Blackstone</h2>

<p>In 1819 Borrow left school and was articled to a solicitor in Norwich, which meant sitting behind a tall deal desk for about eight hours a day copying deeds. He was sixteen. He was, by every account, not suited to it. A later editor of <em>Lavengro</em> put the situation in one line that is worth the whole of this article: <strong>"He learnt Welsh when he should have been reading Blackstone."</strong></p>

<p>Blackstone's <em>Commentaries</em> were the foundation of English legal study. Welsh was not on any syllabus at all. Borrow learned Welsh at the desk, in the gaps, instead of the law, and he was honest about it in his own words: "By adopting the law, I had not ceased to be Lavengro" - the name his Romani friends had given him, which he glossed as Word-master.</p>

<p>What makes the Welsh episode more than an anecdote is that he later described exactly how it went, and the description is a first-person account of cross-language transfer, written a century and a half before the term existed:</p>

<blockquote>I was studying Welsh, and I fell in with Ab Gwilym by no very strange chance... If I remember right, I found the language a difficult one; in mastering it, however, I derived unexpected assistance from what of Irish remained in my head, and I soon found that they were cognate dialects, springing from some old tongue which itself, perhaps, had sprung from one much older.</blockquote>

<p>The Irish he had bought with a pack of cards in 1815 was still in his head in 1819, and it did work he had not planned for. He also recorded why he kept going, and it was not discipline: a fourteenth-century Welsh poet named Dafydd ap Gwilym, whose work he found in a "short thick volume, stuffed full with his immortal imaginings". He had found a text he wanted to read in the language, which is a different engine from a course he felt he ought to finish.</p>

<h2>The Nickname That Became the Title</h2>

<p>The Romani came later and came from the road. Borrow spent years among English Romanichal families, and in <em>Lavengro</em> he describes the moment his status changed among them. He had been called <em>Sap-engro</em>, which he glosses as snake-master, after a story about a viper. Then, after he had been studying their language and their customs with obvious speed, the man he calls Jasper Petulengro renamed him:</p>

<blockquote>"We'll no longer call you Sap-engro, brother," said he; "but rather Lav-engro, which in the language of the gorgios meaneth Word-master."</blockquote>

<p>It is a nickname from a traveling community, given to a Norfolk solicitor's clerk because he had bothered to learn their language properly rather than merely collecting words from it.</p>

<h2>Walking, and the Bible Society</h2>

<p>In 1825 he began the first of his long European journeys, walking through France and Germany. Walking is the operative word. He traveled without much money, on foot, which put him in the villages and the markets and the roadside camps rather than in the salons, and it meant that every language he met was met in use. Russia, Portugal, Spain and Morocco followed over the next years.</p>

<p>In August 1833 he left Norwich for St Petersburg, employed by the British and Foreign Bible Society to supervise a translation of the Bible into Manchu. He had told the Society, in a letter that is a model of the modest claim: "I possess some acquaintance with the Russian, being able to read without much difficulty any printed Russian book." Read without much difficulty. Not speak, not write - read. He was describing a real and testable competence rather than a fantasy of fluency, and the distinction matters, because it is the level most adult self-study actually reaches first and most learners refuse to count.</p>

<p>While in Russia he called on Pushkin, who was out; Borrow left two copies of his translations of Pushkin's work, and Pushkin later expressed regret at missing him. In 1835, back in England, Borrow published <em>Targum: or Metrical Translations from Thirty Languages and Dialects</em>. Thirty. That is his own count, in his own title page, in a book anyone could check.</p>

<h2>Spain, and Two Translations Nobody Wanted</h2>

<p>From November 1835 he spent nearly five years in Spain distributing and printing scripture, and the book that came out of it, <em>The Bible in Spain</em> (1843), made him famous - four editions in three volumes in the year of publication, and named in the House of Commons by Sir Robert Peel. It is a travel book with a language book buried inside it: five years of arguing, preaching, bargaining and being arrested in Spanish, Galician, Basque and Calo.</p>

<p>His most remarkable linguistic act of the period was not the travelogue. He translated the Gospel of Luke into Basque and into Calo, the Romani of Spain, and both were published in 1838 - and both were then banned from personal sale, with only public libraries permitted to hold copies. He had learned two minority languages well enough to render scripture into them, and the market for the result was a few shelves in a few institutions.</p>

<p>This is the part of Borrow's career that gets skipped in favor of the walking and the fighting. He was not a collector of languages for their own sake. He used them as instruments on texts: Schiller in 1821, Danish ballads in 1826, the Manchu Bible in the 1830s, Luke in Basque and Calo in 1838, thirty languages in <em>Targum</em>. Translation was his examination, and it is a far harder one than a conversation.</p>

<h2>The Later Books, and the Welsh He Finally Learned Properly</h2>

<p>He married in 1840 and settled at Oulton in Suffolk, and there wrote the books he is remembered for: <em>The Zincali</em> (1841) on the Romani of Spain, <em>Lavengro</em> (1851), <em>The Romany Rye</em> (1857), <em>Wild Wales</em> (1862), and in 1874 <em>Romano Lavo-Lil</em>, a word-book of the Anglo-Romani dialect. <em>Wild Wales</em> is the record of a walking tour taken in his fifties, in a country whose language he had begun at a desk at sixteen and had never stopped returning to.</p>

<p>He died at Oulton on 26 July 1881 and is buried with his wife in Brompton Cemetery, London. His reputation was odd for decades - readers could not decide how much of <em>Lavengro</em> was fact - and Arthur Conan Doyle pastiched his style in 1913 in a story actually called "Borrow"-ed Scenes. The languages, at least, were not invented. Taylor's letter of 1821, the Bible Society's files, the title pages and the surviving Basque and Calo gospels are all checkable.</p>

<h2>What His Method Actually Consisted Of</h2>

<p>Strip away the walking and the eccentricity and four practices remain, and all four are available to anybody with a library card and a phone.</p>

<h3>1. He learned from texts he wanted to read, not from courses he ought to finish</h3>

<p>German came from Schiller's play. Welsh survived because of Dafydd ap Gwilym. Danish produced a book of ballads. In every case the text was the reason and the grammar was the servant. A learner who has a book they are desperate to read in the language has a motive that does not depend on motivation, and that is the only kind that lasts past February.</p>

<h3>2. He traded for instruction</h3>

<p>The pack of cards is the cleanest example, but the pattern repeats: William Taylor in Norwich, Richard Carey at Clonmel, Romani families on the road, a series of employers who needed a language he happened to have. He was never too proud to barter, and he understood that a fluent speaker who wants something from you is a better teacher than a patient one who does not.</p>

<h3>3. He translated as a test</h3>

<p>Reading a book and saying you understand it is unfalsifiable. Producing a translation is not. Every stage of Borrow's career is marked by an artifact somebody else could inspect, which is why we can still check his claims. A learner who translates one page a week has a weekly examination that marks itself.</p>

<h3>4. He used what he already had</h3>

<p>The Irish bought for cards helped him with Welsh, and he noticed the relationship himself and said so. Most learners treat each new language as a fresh start and lose the leverage. Borrow treated the ones he had as tools for the next one.</p>

<h2>What to Take From a Difficult Man</h2>

<p>Borrow is not an attractive model in every respect. He was quarrelsome, he exaggerated when it suited him, and the twelve languages of 1821 were almost certainly reading knowledge of varying depth rather than twelve conversational command. He would have been the first to admit the difference, because he was the one who wrote "I possess some acquaintance with the Russian" rather than claiming to speak it.</p>

<p>What remains useful is the shape of the thing. A boy the school wrote off as stupid learned a language for a deck of cards, learned another at a desk he was supposed to be using for law, and spent the next fifty years turning languages into texts. None of that required an institution. It required a text worth reading, a speaker worth talking to, and the willingness to produce something checkable at the end.</p>

<blockquote>The school measured him on the curriculum it had. He measured himself on the books he could read, and he kept the receipts.</blockquote>

